---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { pick } from '../data/faq';
---
<Guide
  page="downloads"
  lede="Cupola puts qBittorrent, Transmission, SABnzbd and NZBGet in one list on your phone. When a download gets stuck, it tells you why and offers the fix."
  screen="activity"
  services={['qbittorrent', 'transmission', 'sabnzbd', 'nzbget', 'prowlarr']}
  questions={pick('away', 'certs', 'widgets')}
  related={['arr', 'homelab', 'install']}
>
  <h2>One queue for every client</h2>
  <p>All your downloads in one live list, with pause and resume on every row. If a client stops updating, Cupola says so instead of showing old speeds.</p>

  <h2>Stuck downloads, with the fix</h2>
  <p><em>Needs you</em> lists what your stack cannot fix by itself, each with the right next step:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Try another download</strong>, when this one can never finish. Radarr or Sonarr drops it and looks for another.</li>
    <li><strong>Look for peers again</strong>, when nobody with the file is online right now.</li>
    <li><strong>Review</strong>, when files arrived and need you to say where they go.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The same problem on many downloads shows as one line with a count. The service's own message is under <em>Why?</em></p>

  <h2>Each client in full</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>qBittorrent</strong>: filters, search, files and trackers; pause, force-start, recheck; speed limits, categories and tags.</li>
    <li><strong>Transmission</strong>: labels, reordering, speed limits, and adding a <code>.torrent</code> file.</li>
    <li><strong>SABnzbd</strong>: queue, history and servers; pause one job or everything; add by URL.</li>
    <li><strong>NZBGet</strong>: queue, history, feeds and messages; pause, add and delete.</li>
    <li><strong>Prowlarr</strong>: your indexers and their stats; add, test and sync them to your apps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Widgets and alerts</h2>
  <p>A <em>Downloads</em> widget on iOS and Android, and on the iPhone lock screen. Turn on <em>Needs you</em> to get a notification when something breaks. Your phone checks your services itself, so the system decides how often.</p>
</Guide>
